## The Compiler Prevents Dangling Pointers

A **dangling reference** points to memory that has been freed. XIOM's lifetime system makes dangling references a compile-time error.

### The Problem (in C)

```c
// C code (dangerous)
// int* bad() {
//   int x = 42;
//   return &x;  // x dies — pointer dangles!
// }
```

### How XIOM Prevents It

```xiom
// INVALID: won't compile
// fn bad() -> &Int {
//   let x = 42;
//   return &x;  // COMPILER ERROR: x doesn't live long enough
// }
```

The compiler tracks lifetimes — a reference can't outlive the value it points to.

### Valid: Return from Parameter

```xiom
fn first_element(v: &Vec[Int]) -> &Int {
  return &v[0];  // OK — reference lives as long as v
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let data = Vec[Int]();
  data.push(42);
  let first = first_element(&data);
  // first is valid as long as data is alive
  return *first;
}
```

### Valid: Return Owned Value

```xiom
fn create() -> Vec[Int] {
  let v = Vec[Int]();
  v.push(42);
  return v;  // ownership returned — no dangling reference
}
```

### Static Lifetimes

```xiom
const GREETING: Str = "Hello";

fn get_greeting() -> &Str {
  return &GREETING;  // OK — const has static lifetime (lives forever)
}
```

### Diagnostic

If you get a lifetime error:
1. The value doesn't live long enough for the reference
2. Either: return ownership instead, or restructure code
3. Never: use unsafe to bypass — that creates actual dangling references

